  "description": "A Canaanite merchant vessel sailed the eastern Mediterranean around 1300 BC, carrying one of the richest cargos ever found from the Bronze Age: ten tons of Cypriot copper, a ton of tin, Egyptian glass, ivory, gold, and luxury goods from across the Near East and Aegean. Bound most likely for a Mycenaean port, she sank off the rocky coast of Uluburun and lay undisturbed for over 3,300 years.\n\nWhy the Uluburun Ship Matters\nIt is the oldest known seagoing shipwreck with a largely intact cargo and the earliest clear evidence of advanced mortise-and-tenon hull construction. More than any other single discovery, Uluburun shows how deeply interconnected the Late Bronze Age world truly was — a floating crossroads of trade, diplomacy, and technology linking Cyprus, the Levant, Egypt, and the Aegean.",
